'''Tamazgha''' (Imfezzan: ⵜⴰⵎⴰⵣⵖⴰ, ''Tamāzɣa''), officially the '''Taghlan of Tamazgha''' ({{Langx|taq|ⵜⴰⵖⵍⴰⵏ ⵏ ⵜⴰⵎⴰⵣⵖⴰ|Taghlān n Tamāzɣa}}), and alternatively known as the '''Tamazghan Realm''', is a sovereign state located within the [[Central Kaftia]], occupying the majority of the [[Central Kaftian Desert|Tanaruf n-Azref]]. It borders the [[Bight of Ayorin]] to the west, [[COUNTRY]] to the north, the [[Sea of Hilal]] to the east, and [[Ayorin]], [[Enfolo]] and [[Desket]] to the south. It is the largest Imfezzan-majority nation in [[Adonia]] and serves as the cultural, spiritual, and political heartland of the [[Imfezzan peoples]]. Spanning over 2.4 million square kilometers, Tamazgha is one of the largest countries by area in Adonia, though its population remains sparse due to the harsh desert environment.

Tamazgha is a unique political entity known as a [[Taghlan]]—a [[W:Decentralization|decentralized]] confederal monarchy in which the [[Amenukal]] ({{Literal translation|supreme chief|emperor}}) presides over a federation of semi-autonomous tribes, clans, city-states, and [[W:Oasis|oasis]] communities. Power is distributed through a layered system of traditional councils called [[Aman]] ({{Literal translation|water|oasis}}, the traditional gathering place), with the [[Grand Aman]] serving as the national legislature. This system blends traditional Imfezzan governance traditions with modern state institutions.

The country is linguistically diverse, home to a broad variety of the [[W:Berber languages|Imfezzan languages]]. The official language is [[Tamanghash language|Tamanghash]], spoken by approximately 60% of the population as a first language and used in government, education, and inter-tribal communication. Regional languages include [[Tazrifat language|Tazrifat]], [[Tashalat language|Tashalat]], [[Taqbalat language|Taqbalat]], [[Tasiwat language|Tasiwat]], [[Tamzabat language|Tamzabat]], [[Tahaggart language|Tahaggart]], and [[Tamattit language|Tamattit]], each associated with distinct geographic regions and tribal confederations.

Tamazgha's economy is driven by mineral extraction (particularly [[W:Rare earth elements|rare earth elements]], phosphates, and [[W:Uranium|uranium]]), solar energy exports, date cultivation, [[W:Salt mining|salt mining]], and a growing tourism sector centered on prehistoric rock art sites, ancient [[W:Caravan|caravan]] cities, and desert ecotourism. The nation possesses the largest untapped solar energy potential in Adonia, and has invested heavily in [[W:Photovoltaic|photovoltaic]] infrastructure for both domestic use and export.

==Etymology==
{{Main|Name of Tamazgha}}

The name ''Tamazɣa'' (Imfezzan: ⵜⴰⵎⴰⵣⵖⴰ) derives from the Imfezzan root MZƔ (''mazgh''), meaning "free people" or "noble ones." The prefix ''ta-'' and suffix ''-a'' form a feminine collective noun, yielding ''Tamazɣa''—"land of the free people" or "land of the noble ones." This etymology reflects the Imfezzan self-conception as a people who have never been permanently subjugated by foreign powers.

The term has been used since antiquity to refer to the collective homeland of the Imfezzan peoples across the desert region. Prior to unification in 1962, "Tamazgha" existed as a cultural and geographic concept rather than a political entity, encompassing the vast territory inhabited by Imfezzan-speaking tribes from the northern escarpments to the southern dunes.

The official name ''Taghlan of Tamazgha'' combines the political structure (''Taghlan'', "the Confederation") with the geographic and cultural identity (''Tamazɣa''). In common usage, both domestically and internationally, the country is referred to simply as "Tamazgha."

===Taghlan===
{{Main|Taghlan}}

''Taghlan'' (Imfezzan: ⵜⴰⵖⵍⴰⵏ, ''Taɣlan'') derives from the Imfezzan root ƔLN (''ghalan''), meaning "to gather, unite, bring together." The term reflects the fundamental nature of the Imfezzan state—a voluntary confederation of tribes, clans, and oasis communities united under a single Amenukal. Unlike a [[W:Empire|centralized empire]], a Taghlan implies a gathering of equals who retain internal autonomy while binding themselves together for mutual defense, trade, and dispute resolution. Before unification, the term ''taghlan'' referred to any temporary alliance of nomadic groups during times of drought, war, or pilgrimage. The modern Taghlan of Tamazgha transformed this ad-hoc institution into a permanent political structure.

===Aman===
{{Main|Aman (council)}}

In Imfezzan languages, ''aman'' literally means "water." In desert societies, water sources (wells, oases, springs) are the traditional gathering places where travelers, nomads, and settled communities meet to exchange news, settle disputes, and conduct trade. The well is where decisions are made—thus "Aman" evolved to mean both "water" and "council/assembly."

An Aman is a deliberative council where participants gather as equals to discuss matters of common concern. The metaphor is deliberate: just as water sustains life, the council sustains the social order. The [[Grand Aman]] (''Aman Amqran'') is the national legislature where representatives from all tribes and regions gather. '''Proverb:''' ''"Aman d-tidawt, Aman d-tidawt n-ugdud."'' ("Water is life; the Council is the life of the people.")

==History==
{{Main|History of Tamazgha}}

===Prehistory===
{{Main|Prehistoric Tamazgha}}

[[File:Mathendous giraffes.jpg|thumb|Engraving of [[W:Giraffes|giraffes]] at [[Wadi Tassili]] in the north of Tamagzha.]]
The territory of modern Tamazgha has been inhabited since the [[W:Paleolithic|Paleolithic]] era. Archaeological evidence, including rock art dating back over 12,000 years, indicates that the region once supported a vastly different climate with abundant wildlife, lakes, and [[W:Savanna|savanna]] vegetation. The famous [[Tassili rock paintings]] depict giraffes, [[W:Elephant|elephants]], [[W:Crocodiles|crocodiles]], and cattle herds, suggesting a "Green Desert" period during the [[W:Holocene climatic optimum|Holocene climatic optimum]].

As the climate gradually aridified beginning around 5000 BCE, populations adapted through pastoral nomadism, oasis agriculture, and trans-Saharan trade. The domestication of the [[W:Camel|camel]] around 1000 BCE revolutionized desert transportation and enabled the emergence of long-distance trade networks connecting [[Sudanese Kaftia]] with [[Southwestern Kaftia]] and the [[Mesogean basin|Mesogean world]].

==Geography==
{{Main|Geography of Tamazgha}}

Tamazgha is a transcontinental nation that spans the entirety of central Kaftia between the [[Aethiopian Ocean]] and the [[Kendriyan ocean]] occupying a vast expanse of desert terrain in [[Central Kaftia]]. With an area of {{convert|2406517.19|km2|sqmi|abbr=on}}, it is one of the largest countries in Adonia by land area. The country is characterized by extreme aridity, with annual rainfall averaging less than 100 mm in most regions and some areas receiving virtually no precipitation for years at a time.

===Physical Features===

The landscape of Tamazgha is dominated by several distinct geographic zones:

* '''The Great Sand Sea''' (Imfezzan: ''Tanaruf n-Azref''): An immense expanse of [[W:Erg (landform)|erg]] (sand dune fields) covering much of the central and eastern Taghlan, with dunes reaching heights of over {{convert|300|m|ft|abbr=on}} in some areas.

* '''The Highland Escarpments''': A series of rugged plateaus and mountain ranges along the northern frontier, reaching elevations of over {{convert|2000|m|ft|abbr=on}}. These highlands capture occasional orographic rainfall and support more diverse vegetation.

* '''The Oasis Belt''': A chain of depressions and aquifer-fed oases stretching across the central Taghlan, supporting the majority of the sedentary agricultural population.

* '''The Volcanic Highlands''' (''Adrar n-Tidawt''): An ancient volcanic massif in the southeast, featuring dramatic basalt formations, crater lakes, and relatively cooler temperatures.

* '''The Salt Flats''' (''Tazruyt n-Isli''): Expansive [[W:Chott|chott]] (salt pan) formations in the western regions, remnants of ancient lakes that evaporated during the desertification of the desert.

===Climate===

Tamazgha experiences a [[W:Hot desert climate|hot desert climate]] ([[W:Köppen climate classification|Köppen climate classification]] ''BWh''), with extreme temperature variations between day and night. Summer temperatures regularly exceed {{convert|45|°C|°F|abbr=on}} in the interior, while winter nights can approach freezing in the highlands. The northern escarpments receive marginally more precipitation, supporting limited dryland agriculture.

===Flora and Fauna===

Despite the harsh conditions, Tamazgha supports a surprising diversity of desert-adapted life. Native flora includes various species of [[W:Acacia|acacia]], [[W:Tamarisk|tamarisk]], and [[W:Date palm|date palm]] in oasis environments, while the sand seas support drought-resistant grasses and shrubs. Wildlife includes the [[W:Fennec fox|fennec fox]], [[W:Sand cat|sand cat]], [[W:Desert hedgehog|desert hedgehog]], [[W:Addax antelope|addax]], [[W:Dama gazelle|dama gazelle]], and numerous reptile species. Migratory birds pass through the Taghlan along ancient routes, stopping at oasis wetlands.

==Government and politics==
{{Main|Politics of Tamazgha}}

Tamazgha is a [[Taghlan]]—a distinctive form of decentralized confederal monarchy that blends traditional Imfezzan governance structures with modern state institutions. The system is characterized by significant autonomy for constituent tribes and regions, with the central government primarily responsible for defense, foreign relations, inter-tribal dispute resolution, and management of strategic resources.

===Amenukal===
{{Main|Amenukal of Tamazgha}}

The ''Amenukal'' (Imfezzan: ⴰⵎⴻⵏⵓⴽⴰⵍ, "Lord of the Men") is the [[head of state]] and supreme chief of the Taghlan. The position combines elements of a constitutional monarch, a tribal paramount chief, and a spiritual leader. The Amenukal serves as:

* Symbol of national unity and continuity
* Guardian of Imfezzan traditions and customary law
* Final arbiter in inter-tribal disputes
* [[Commander-in-chief]] of the Taghlan Defense Forces
* Representative of Tamazgha in foreign affairs

The Amenukal is selected by an [[W:Electoral college|electoral college]] composed of the heads of major tribal confederations, the [[Council of Elders]], and representatives from major cities and oasis communities. The position is not strictly hereditary, though dynastic succession is common within ruling families. The current Amenukal is [[Tadala II of Tamazgha|Tadala II]], who ascended to the position in 2015.

The feminine form of Amenukal is ''Amenukalt'' (Imfezzan: ⴰⵎⴻⵏⵓⴽⴰⵍⵜ):

