==Languages==
{{Main|Languages of Tamazgha}}

Tamazgha is a [[multilingualism|multilingual]] nation with seven officially recognized Imfezzan languages. The constitution designates [[Tamanghash language|Tamanghash]] as the official language of government and inter-tribal communication, while regional languages are protected and promoted within their respective territories.

Tamanghash (ⵜⴰⵎⴰⵏⵖⴰⵛ) is spoken by approximately 6.2 million people as a first language, with additional speakers as a second language. It serves as the ''[[W:Lingua franca|lingua franca]]'' of the Taghlan and is used in education, media, government administration, and commerce. Tamanghash is written in both the indigenous [[W:Tifinagh|Tifinagh]] script and the [[W:Latin alphabet|Mesogean alphabet]].

==Culture==
{{Main|Culture of Tamazgha}}

===Music and dance===

Imfezzan music is characterized by [[W:Call and response (music)|call-and-response]] vocals, [[W:Polyphony|polyphonic]] singing, and distinctive instruments including the ''[[W:Imzad|imzad]]'' (single-stringed fiddle played by women), ''[[W:Tendit|tendit]]'' (lute), and various drums and percussion instruments. The ''Ahal'' is a traditional musical gathering featuring poetry, music, and dance, central to courtship and social bonding.

Traditional dances include the ''Izlan'' (sword dance) performed by men and various group dances performed at weddings and festivals.

===Art and crafts===

Imfezzan material culture includes:

* '''Jewelry''': Silver jewelry with distinctive geometric designs, often incorporating [[W:Coral|coral]], [[W:Amber|amber]], and semi-precious stones
* '''Textiles''': Woven blankets, tents, and garments with symbolic patterns indicating tribal affiliation and social status
* '''Leatherworking''': Traditional saddles, bags, and sandals
* '''Pottery''': Hand-built ceramics in oasis communities
* '''Metalwork''': Traditional weapons, tools, and decorative objects

===Cuisine===

[[Tamazghan cuisine]] reflects the desert environment and nomadic heritage:

* '''Taguella''': Traditional flatbread baked in sand or on hot stones
* '''Méchoui''': Roasted whole lamb or goat, served at celebrations
* '''Couscous''': Steamed semolina with vegetables and meat
* '''Dates''': Staple food consumed fresh, dried, or processed into syrup
* '''Camel milk and cheese''': Important nutritional sources for nomadic populations
* '''Mint tea''': Central to hospitality rituals

===Clothing===

Traditional Imfezzan clothing includes:

* [[W:Tagelmust|Tagelmust]] (men): Indigo-dyed turban and veil, often leaving only eyes visible
* '''Melfa''' (women): Wrapped fabric garment in various colors
* '''Takchita''' (women): Layered dress for formal occasions
* '''[[W:Babouches|Babouches]]''': Traditional leather slippers

The famous "[[W:Tuareg#Name|blue men]]" of the desert derive their name from the indigo dye that stains their skin, a mark of distinction among certain noble tribes.

